{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe underestimation of volatility: The model consistently predicts a smooth, monotonic trend (either flat or slightly upward) while true outcomes exhibit high-amplitude spikes (e.g., rising from ~70 to ~76) followed by sharp corrections. Apply a volatility multiplier to forecast ranges rather than point estimates.",
    "Implement aggressive mean reversion after sentiment spikes: When sentiment history shows a value of 1.00 (positive) or -1.00 (negative), the model fails to anticipate the subsequent price reversal. If a positive sentiment spike is followed by a price surge, immediately reduce the forecast for the following 5-10 days to account for the inevitable pullback.",
    "Eliminate momentum bias in flat markets: The model exhibits a persistent upward drift bias (e.g., forecasting 72.91 when the true mean is ~70.5) even when recent history is flat or declining. Reset the baseline forecast to the 18-day rolling average rather than extrapolating the last day's value.",
    "Adjust for lag in trend reversal: The model reacts too slowly to trend changes, often continuing to forecast increases after the true price has already peaked and begun falling. Introduce a lag-correction factor that shifts the forecast curve leftward by 2-3 days when the 3-day price momentum turns negative.",
    "Discard sentiment signals that do not correlate with immediate price action: In several instances, high sentiment scores (1.00) were followed by price drops rather than rises. Treat sentiment as a volatility indicator rather than a directional signal; use it to widen forecast confidence intervals rather than to shift the mean direction."
  ]
}
